   342	 \gfPool[\cdot],\attestations[\cdot],\\
   343	 &\genuine[\cdot],\lease[\cdot],\fastWrite[\cdot],\latestConfirmed,
   344	 F,J,h_j,\hmax).
   345	\end{split}
   346	\]
   347	The first line holds the tree, the block states, and the pools.  The
   348	second line holds the SA fields and the FG caches of~\DC{5}.  The
   349	validator record~$\Lambda$ of Section~\ref{sec:record} is not part of
   350	the store.
   351	
   352	\paragraph{Trees.}  $\Sigma.\T$ is DC's admitted tree, with the same
   353	name and the same rules as in~\DC{7}.  It is parent-closed and
   354	cumulative: a new block enters it only if it extends~$F$, and no block
   355	leaves it~\cite{DC}.  A node never computes the state of, or unpacks, a
   356	block that does not extend its~$F$.  The \emph{active view} is
   357	\[
   358	 \act(\Sigma)=\{B\in\Sigma.\T: B\preceq\Sigma.F\ \text{or}\ \Sigma.F\preceq B\}.
   359	\]
   360	The SA base reads only the active view: the gate, release, resolution,
   361	saved-result tests, leases, and the confirmation walk~\cite[\S2]{FB}.
   362	Thus a node ignores every block that conflicts with its finality.  The
   363	FG functions read $\Sigma.\T$ as in~\DC{7}; their filters already
   364	require $\Sigma.F\preceq B$.  The caches $\Sigma.\hmax$ and the contest
   365	test read the cumulative $\Sigma.\T$, as the caches of~\DC{5} do.  A
   366	block of $\Sigma.\T$ outside the active view with height above
   367	$\Sigma.h_F$ needs a slashable conflict (\textsf{fg.finalLock}).  A
   368	node can delete blocks outside the active view; with $a\leq f$ this
   369	changes no output.  The confirmation walk starts at genesis.  The FJ
   370	viability and band filter is a further filter for HEAD, proposal, and
   371	FG duties; it does not restrict the SA confirmation walk.
   372	
   373	\paragraph{Block states and FG caches.}  $\Sigma.\sigma[B]$ is the
   374	immutable post-state of an admitted block~$B$, the chain state
   375	of~\DC{4}:
   376	\[
   377	 \sigma=(L,s,h,T_h,\nj,\targetParticipation,\progressBit,\finalityBit,
   378	 J,h_j,F,h_F).
   379	\]
   380	$\sigma.J$, $\sigma.F$, $\sigma.T_h$, and $\sigma.L$ are blocks.  The
   381	arrays $\sigma.\targetParticipation$, $\sigma.\progressBit$, and
   382	$\sigma.\finalityBit$ hold one Boolean for each identity in~$V$.  Their
   383	quorum sets are derived when used:
   384	\[
   385	 \begin{aligned}
   386	  \Qtarget(\sigma)&=\{i\in V:\sigma.\targetParticipation[i]\},&
   387	  \Qprog(\sigma)&=\{i\in V:\sigma.\progressBit[i]\},\\
   388	  \Qfin(\sigma)&=\{i\in V:\sigma.\finalityBit[i]\}.&&
   389	 \end{aligned}
   390	\]
   391	They are sets of fixed identities, not row counts and not per-round
   392	collections.  The progress and target bits persist across rounds until
   393	the height advances; the finality bits are cleared only by a new
   394	justification.  The node computes $\sigma[B]$ only when it admits~$B$,
   395	from the parent's state; the parent of an admitted block is admitted.
   396	$(\Sigma.F,\Sigma.J,\Sigma.h_j,\Sigma.\hmax)$ are the live caches
   397	of~\DC{5}: $\Sigma.F$ is the accepted finalization, $(\Sigma.J,\Sigma.h_j)$
   398	is the lex-greatest justification with $\Sigma.F\preceq\Sigma.J$ over the
   399	admitted states, and $\Sigma.\hmax$ is the maximum of all state heights
   400	admitted so far, as in~\DC{5}.  All four are monotone at one node: $\Sigma.F$ moves only to a
   401	descendant, $(\Sigma.h_j,\Sigma.J)$ only up in lex order, and
   402	$\Sigma.\hmax$ only up~\LDP{4.2}.  The FG root, the band floor, the
   403	viability test, and finality acceptance read these caches, as
   404	in~\cite{DC}.
   405	
   406	\paragraph{Pools.}  $\Sigma.\gfPool[r]$ holds the processed slot-$r$
   407	Goldfish votes, as in~\SAS.  It \emph{admits} a vote only when its slot
   408	is the current slot or the previous slot, and it admits at most two
   409	distinct votes per seat.  It keeps every vote that it admitted; there is
   410	no pruning rule (Section~\ref{sec:open}).  The store stamps each block
   411	and each vote with $\Sigma.\timestamp$, the time of the last tick
   412	before processing.  A vote $\mathit{vote}=(i,r,H)$ has the resolution
   413	time $\max\{\Sigma.\timestamp(\mathit{vote}),\Sigma.\timestamp(H)\}$
   414	when $H\in\act(\Sigma)$ and $H.\slot\leq r$, and $+\infty$ otherwise.
   415	A missing or incompatible head cannot supply support, including in frozen
   416	and carried-support views.  Resolution is tested against the current
   417	active view at every read; a saved resolved flag cannot bypass this
   418	test.  Keep the vote's original signature and receipt stamp.  It still
   419	counts for the two-vote cap, late participation, and equivocation.  A
   420	stored finalized ancestor still resolves at its original time.
   421	$\Sigma.\attestations[r]$ holds the processed round-$r$ FG rows, in
   422	full.  It admits a row only when its round is in the window
   423	$\max\{0,r_{\rm now}-w_F\},\dots,r_{\rm now}$, and it keeps at most two
   424	distinct rows per identity and round.  Two rows that differ in any
   425	field are distinct: without a safe field, the pool removes only
   426	duplicates of the full row~\ADJ{2}.  No rule reads a row's time of
   427	receipt; the pool serves inclusion only.
   428	
   429	\paragraph{SA fields.}  $\Sigma.\genuine[r]$ holds the genuine
   430	result of slot~$r$, a block or $\bot$; the gate at~$d_r$ evaluates it
   431	once, also when the result is empty or vetoed.  Reconciliation can
   432	replace an incompatible result by $\bot$.  Its slot stays evaluated;
   433	the gate is never run again.  A compatible result keeps its original
   434	block, including when that block is below~$F$.  $\Sigma.\lease[B]$ is
   435	the deadline of the lease on~$B$, or $\bot$.  A lease expires at the
   436	first tick at or after its deadline, and it is released at~$q_r$ when
   437	$\tau$ or more seats conflict with it in the slot-$r$ pool.
   438	$\Sigma.\latestConfirmed$ is the SA record~$S$.  These rules are those
   439	of~\SAS, with reconciliation and the output rule of~\cite[\S2]{FB}.
   440	The live lease targets lie on one chain (the
   441	whole-result guard), and every non-genesis ancestor of a live lease
   442	target is a live lease target: a write covers every admissible prefix,
   443	an ancestor has a deadline at least as late, and a vote that conflicts
   444	with an ancestor also conflicts with the descendant~\LDP{1}.
   445	$\Sigma.\fastWrite[r]$ is new: the set of blocks to which the gate
   446	at~$d_r$ wrote or renewed a lease by the fast test~\FAST{3.1}.  The
   447	gate creates it once, as the empty set when the result is empty or
   448	vetoed.  Reconciliation removes incompatible targets from every such set.  A slow renewal alone does not put a block in it.  A renewal
   449	counts also when the maximum leaves the stored deadline unchanged.
   450	
   451	\paragraph{Cached and derived.}  Besides the tree, the stamps, and
   452	the pools, the store caches these fields: the block states
   453	$\Sigma.\sigma$; the SA fields $\Sigma.\genuine$, $\Sigma.\lease$,
   454	$\Sigma.\fastWrite$, and $\Sigma.\latestConfirmed$; and the FG caches
   455	$\Sigma.F$, $\Sigma.J$, $\Sigma.h_j$, and $\Sigma.\hmax$.  The node
   456	derives all other quantities when it uses them, from timestamps
   457	compared against past instants: the contest bit, the FG root, the band
   458	floor, a viable tree, a filtered tree, a processed-block view, the live
   459	root, a frozen pool, an early or late pool, a score, a support count, a
   460	release count, the source~$\Cfg$, and a head.
   461	
   462	\paragraph{Finality reconciliation.}  On each $F$ advance, the active
   463	view shrinks to the blocks compatible with the new~$F$; $\Sigma.\T$
   464	stays cumulative.  Delete incompatible leases and
   465	their targets from all $\fastWrite$ sets, and empty incompatible saved
   466	genuine results without clearing their evaluated slots.  Set $S:=F$
   467	when $F\not\preceq S$.  Keep signed vote and row pools, vote and block
   468	stamps, the validator record~$\Lambda$, and~$\hmax$.
   469	A compatible lease below~$F$ can remain until its usual expiry or
   470	release; it cannot veto a descendant of~$F$.  Never move a conflicting
   471	lease to a new target or replace a saved result by~$F$.  The assignment
   472	to~$S$ creates no lease, genuine result, or fast write~\cite[\S2]{FB}.
   473	
   474	\paragraph{Initialization.}
   475	\[
   476	 \begin{gathered}
   477	 \Sigma.t=\Sigma.s=0,\quad \Sigma.\T=\{\genesis\},\quad
   478	 \Sigma.\timestamp(\genesis)=0,\quad \Sigma.\hmax=1,\\
   479	 \Sigma.F=\Sigma.J=\Sigma.\latestConfirmed=\genesis,
   480	 \quad \Sigma.h_j=0,
   481	 \end{gathered}
   482	\]
   483	every pool is empty, and $\Sigma.\genuine$, $\Sigma.\lease$, and
   484	$\Sigma.\fastWrite$ have no entries.  $\Sigma.\sigma[\genesis]$ is the
   485	initial chain state of~\DC{4}:
   486	\[
   487	 \sigma.L=\sigma.T_h=\sigma.J=\sigma.F=\genesis,\quad
   488	 \sigma.s=0,\quad \sigma.h=1,\quad \sigma.h_j=\sigma.h_F=0,\quad
   489	 \sigma.\nj=\mathsf{false},
   490	\]
   491	and every array entry is false.
   492	
   493	\paragraph{Input validation.}  Every handler of
   494	Figure~\ref{alg:store} receives only a \emph{checked} object.  The
   495	ingress check drops an object that fails it; a dropped object is not
   496	accepted and has no effect.  The check is:
   497	\begin{itemize}[nosep]
   498	\item \emph{vote} $(i,s,B)$: $i\in\{1,\dots,n\}$, and seat~$i$ of
   499	  slot~$s$ signs it.  The head~$B$ can be unknown;
   500	\item \emph{row}: $\valindex\in V$, the identity $\valindex$ signs it,
   778	  \Comment{new, \FAST{4}; user-facing}
