     1	# FG-SA with a finality-filtered SA base
     2	
     3	_2026-09-28. Paper analysis. No spec or Lean change._
     4	
     5	**Recommendation: adopt the filtered base with the rules in §2.** Clean
     6	safety and P1 keep their present fault range, `a<h`, and their present
     7	timing premises. Recovery keeps `a≤f` and its other present premises.
     8	The proof needs admission-or-finality case splits in place of unconditional
     9	raw-path transport. There is no forcing execution here that requires a
    10	client to process a block that conflicts with its finality.
    11	
    12	There is an important limit. An arbitrary local lease is not a P1-armed
    13	block. A private finalization can conflict with a local lease and with a
    14	local stable record after an asynchronous prefix, even at `a≤f`. Section 5
    15	gives exact executions. Finality must then take priority. P1 excludes that
    16	prefix; it requires healthy delivery from genesis before the outage.
    17	
    18	```text
    19	┌──────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┐
    20	│ Claim or rule                    │ Verdict      │ Scope or required change             │
    21	├──────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
    22	│ Ignore F-conflicting blocks      │ HOLDS        │ One admitted tree; §2.               │
    23	│ Clean safety and 6Δ service      │ HOLDS        │ All a<h; healthy from genesis.       │
    24	│ Fast and slow P1                 │ HOLDS        │ All a<h; original lag and budget.    │
    25	│ Any local lease survives F moves │ FAILS        │ N1, also at n=4,a=1.                 │
    26	│ Unconditional raw-path transfer  │ FAILS        │ N2; use admission-or-coverage.       │
    27	│ Release and slow-history proofs  │ NEEDS-CHANGE │ Retained paths or finality coverage. │
    28	│ F ⪯ exposed stable S             │ HOLDS        │ All a, by the output rule.           │
    29	│ F ⪯ untouched SA record          │ FAILS        │ N1; admission alone is insufficient. │
    30	│ Recovery and healing             │ HOLDS        │ a≤f; proof changes in §6.            │
    31	│ 996Δ / 1041Δ reference bounds    │ HOLDS        │ Same recovery / strong recurrence.   │
    32	└──────────────────────────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘
    33	```
    34	
    35	Here HOLDS means a paper implication with the stated premises, not a
    36	machine-checked FG-SA theorem. NEEDS-CHANGE identifies a rule or proof
    37	interface that cannot be copied literally. FAILS refers to the exact
    38	statement in the row, not to a stronger claim about the whole protocol.
    39	
    40	## 1. Authorities and the DC comparison
    41	
    42	The checked FG-SA spec is [fgsa-protocol.tex](../spec/fgsa-protocol.tex),
    43	SHA-256 `1c6952b6e6ef92bf9111121f0fb283671af6ce936b6db3c33d056b9dda35ffcd`.
    44	The main proof sources are [r158 §§4–7](alfs-redteam.md),
    45	[r159 §1](alfs-fixes.md), and [r160 §§3–4](receipt-relay.md).
    46	They correct some stronger statements in [LDP](fgsa-live-dc-port.md).
    47	In particular, `h>2a` is not a consequence of `a<h`.
    48	
    49	The DC source is [consensus.tex][dc-paper], especially §§5–7. The checked
    50	Lean checkout heads are:
    51	
    52	```text
    53	available-chain:    12217fbc38827ef31f5956988e22d50aa25f841a
    54	verified-consensus: b241081d344555797205f8718eeb23a8a0e3eb4c
    55	```
    56	
    57	These identify the checkouts, not a claim that their working trees are
    58	clean. The SA model delegates to the shared GridCO engine. The new
    59	`AvailableChainSimplexFG/ChainState.lean` also defines the no-NJ,
    60	entry-bound FG fold. It is not an executable combined FG-SA model.
    61	The executions below were checked against the definitions listed here;
    62	the integer counts, deadlines, and certificate folds were also checked
    63	in memory. No Lean proof or full-run simulator was added or run.
    64	
    65	The specific executable checks use [SA resolution and scoring][sa-score],
    66	[confirmation and release][sa-release], [the guard and lease writes][sa-gate],
    67	[the DC attestation fold and height-event order][dc-fold], and
    68	[the no-NJ FG fold][fg-fold]. N1 is a combined FG-SA execution, not a
    69	claim that DC's different SG duties produce the same messages.
    70	
    71	### 1.1 What DC actually does
    72	
    73	1. **Admission and receipt.** DC refuses a new block unless `F⪯B`, before
    74	   it unpacks that block's votes and rows. The receipt exclusion is
    75	   narrower: `NamedReceipt.excludes` excludes a block *incompatible* with
    76	   `F`. It never excludes a vote or row. An already stored finalized
    77	   ancestor remains available. See [paper lines 1709–1730][dc-admission],
    78	   [`on_block_using`][dc-handler], and [`NamedReceipt.excludes`][dc-excludes].
    79	   DC's tree is cumulative: refusal of new blocks is not itself a rule
    80	   that deletes all previously admitted conflicting blocks.
    81	
    82	2. **Resolution and evidence.** An unknown vote head does not resolve.
    83	   Its signed vote can still occupy a pool position and expose an
    84	   equivocation. SG additionally tests compatibility with current `F`
    85	   when it interprets a vote's safe block. A finalized ancestor keeps
    86	   its original head; it is not rewritten to `F`. See
    87	   [`resolved`][dc-resolved], [`bodyReady`][dc-ready], and DC §6.2.
    88	
    89	3. **Saved grades.** Each grade is computed once. On an F advance, DC
    90	   replaces its saved block by its deepest ancestor compatible with
    91	   the new F. A missing grade remains missing. It does not recompute
    92	   a grade using a different vote population. See DC §6.2,
    93	   [`clipGrade`][dc-clip], and [`clipCache`][dc-cache].
    94	
    95	4. **Positive use and veto.** Grades 2 and 1 use their active prefix
    96	   in the current FG-filtered tree. Grade 1 supplies the full-store
    97	   walk start. Grade 0 is a compatibility veto. If finality or the FG
    98	   root passes a saved grade, its active prefix can be absent; the
    99	   corresponding fallback is the FG root. Grade-2 presence is distinct
   100	   from an absent grade. See DC §§6.3–6.4 and [`frameStableRoot`][dc-root].
   101	
   102	5. **Records and exposed outputs.** DC has no SA leases. Its stable
   103	   record follows the grade-2 root; its confirmation record follows
   104	   confirmation with the stable floor. `advance_confirmed` keeps the
   105	   old record for an ancestor candidate, but can replace it with a
   106	   conflicting candidate outside a safety regime. Finality does not
   107	   rewrite these historical records. The accessors return F when the
   108	   stable record does not extend F, and return stable when the confirmed
   109	   record does not extend stable. Thus the *outputs* are nested even
   110	   when finality moves past or away from an old record. See DC §7,
   111	   [`advance_confirmed`][dc-advance], [`get_stable`, `get_confirmed`][dc-output].
   112	
   113	6. **Permanent signing history.** The validator's first target, timeout,
   114	   and finality lock are not erased when a branch loses admission.
   115	   A finality pair is head-derived and need not have a fresh SG source.
   116	   An emitted height pair does need the prescribed source. The same-row
   117	   finality lock is applied before the height-pair choice. See DC §5.3
   118	   and [`ValidatorClient.lean`][dc-client].
   119	
   120	SA needs analogues of admission, admission-dependent resolution,
   121	finality-aware stored references, the full-store anchor, nested outputs,
   122	and retained signing history. It does not need SG votes, three grades,
   123	SG expiry, grade-0 vetoes, or the grade-2 fallback as new protocol layers.
   124	Its gate guard replaces the relevant local compatibility check. Its
   125	leases and source-transfer proof replace SG's anchor-support argument.
   126	DC's conditional grade delivery is a useful proof pattern, not a theorem
   127	that directly proves SA's P1.
   128	
   129	## 2. Exact filtered-base rule
   130	
   131	Use one admitted, parent-closed tree. Keep genesis and the full ancestor
   132	path of F. The active tree contains only stored blocks compatible with
   133	current F: its ancestors and its descendants. Remove conflicting stored
   134	branches on an F advance. Physical deletion can be deferred, but no base
   135	query may read those branches. This is a finality filter, not the narrower
   136	FJ viability/band filter: the SA confirmation walk still starts at genesis.
   137	
   138	```text
   139	on_block(B):
   140	    perform DC's input, time, duplicate, parent, and F⪯B checks
   141	    if a check fails: return before unpacking B
   142	    compute its immutable chain state; admit B; unpack its objects
   143	    run DC's finality acceptance tests
   144	    if F advances: reconcile_with_finality()
   145	
   146	reconcile_with_finality():
   147	    remove every stored block incompatible with F from the active tree
   148	    delete leases whose targets are incompatible with F
   149	    remove those targets from all fastWrite sets
   150	    replace each saved genuine result incompatible with F by empty
   151	        keep its slot marked evaluated; never rerun that gate
   152	    if not F⪯S: S := F
   153	    keep the signed vote pools, row pools, and validator record
   154	    keep the archival height maximum; do not recompute it from survivors
   155	
   156	gate(s):
   157	    use only the active admitted tree for resolution and confirmation
   158	    save the genuine result once, including empty or vetoed results
   159	    apply the existing whole-result guard and fast/slow lease tests
   160	    apply the existing advance rule for S
   161	    if not F⪯S: S := F
   162	```
   163	
   164	All lease deadlines, the order expire/release/gate/action, and the
   165	definition of `C_fg` remain unchanged. A compatible lease below F can
   166	remain until its usual expiry or release. It cannot veto a descendant
   167	of F. A conflicting lease is deleted, not moved to a different target.
   168	Keep compatible saved results at their original blocks. Do not convert
   169	an old result to F or invent a new confirmation. Emptying a conflicting
   170	saved result can break a slow window; §6 explains why this does not
   171	increase the proved recovery bound.
   172	
   173	The last line of `gate` makes the stored S equal to the DC-style exposed
   174	stable output. Equivalently, keep an internal SA record and expose
   175	`S := record if F⪯record else F`. The recommendation uses the explicit
   176	assignment so a removed block need not remain a live record reference.
   177	A finality assignment to S creates no lease, genuine result, or fast
   178	write. Proofs must distinguish these assignments from gate origins.
   179	
   180	A vote for a missing or removed head has resolution time infinity. Keep
   181	its original signature and receipt stamp: it still counts for the
   182	two-vote cap, late participation, and equivocation. Check this also in
   183	frozen and carried-support views; a cached resolved flag must not retain
   184	support for a deleted head. A finalized ancestor that remains stored
   185	still resolves at its original time. Never delete a vote merely because
   186	its head is excluded. An excluded block supplies no handler calls for
   187	its carried objects, exactly as in DC.
   188	
   189	Keep the FJ live caches, the DC two-arm processed HEAD view, the full-tree
   190	lease anchor, and the admission-based current-proposal merge. This change
   191	does not add an F-basis path, a new HEAD fallback, an SG layer, or a guard
   192	that refuses a valid finalization because of a lease.
   193	
   194	## 3. The admission-or-coverage lemma
   195	
   196	Write `B ~ F` for compatibility. Suppose a proof protects B and has
   197	established `B ~ F_y` at the receiving node y.
   198	
   199	* If `F_y⪯B`, every support head above B extends F_y. Its complete path
   200	  can be admitted. With the existing receipt deadline, the r160
   201	  vote-or-equivocation argument gives exactly the surviving support
   202	  needed at the cutoff. A second vote still acts as evidence even if
   203	  its head cannot resolve.
   204	* If `B⪯F_y`, the receiver's output and every FG-domain HEAD cover B
   205	  through finality. No support-path receipt for B is needed at that
   206	  receiver. Every lease that conflicts with B also conflicts with F_y
   207	  and has been deleted.
   208	
   209	Equality can use either case. An ancestor on an already admitted path
   210	does not need a new acceptance event. If F changes while transport is
   211	pending, apply the cases at the receiving read. A crossing from the first
   212	case into the second discharges the requirement by coverage. It does not
   213	extend the transport deadline.
   214	
   215	This proves the useful replacement for “all source paths arrive.” It
   216	does **not** prove compatibility by itself. Clean/P1 obtain compatibility
   217	from the joint source-history induction in §4. Recovery obtains it from
   218	the high witness and finalized-crossing argument in §6. Use neither proof
   219	outside its premise set.
   220	
   221	In particular, [r160 §3.1](receipt-relay.md) correctly says that DC's
   222	exclusion removes an *unconditional raw-path premise*. It does not give
   223	an execution that needs that premise to preserve the stated claims.
   224	The case split above replaces that premise. Raw release of a lease that
   225	the finality update has already deleted is unnecessary.

