  1760	  - `evidence_mono`: the witnesses stay in the larger set.
  1761	  - `effFrame_of_bits`: a carrier K has `h_j(K) < h(K) ≤ m` (`justified_below_height`), so no pair is above `m−1`. If not `E_J`, no pair is at `m−1` (by `huniq`, a pair at `m−1` names `Jm`, which is `E_J`), so `j < m−1`, `H ≠ j+1`: gap, `lo = m−1`. With `m ≥ 2`, the fallback `(0, genesis)` is also below `m−1`. If `E_J`: `F ⪯ Jm` (`hFJ`), so `(m−1, Jm)` is a candidate and the greatest key (`lexMax_of_candidate`); with `hinj` the selector returns it, so `j = m−1`, `J = Jm`, `H = j+1`. The contest test is exactly `E_C`.
  1762	  - `twoChanges`: by `effFrame_of_bits`, the phase at k is a function of the two bits at k, and the normalized frame is a function of the phase. `hJ` and `hC` make the bits monotone in k. The rank of `(bit_J, bit_C)` under the table is monotone in both bits.
  1763	  - `twoChanges_count`: the normalized frame changes only where the rank changes (`twoChanges`, second part). The rank is monotone with values in `{0, 1, 2}`, so it rises at most twice.
  1764	  - `twoChanges_nested`: `evidence_mono` gives `hJ` and `hC`; then `twoChanges` with `Fs := fun _ => F`.
  1765	  - `Fmove_filtered`: `{c with F := F'}` has the same `H`, `j`, `J`, `contest`, so the same `rootIsJ` and `lo`; its `Z` is `F′` in the F-root phases and `J` in the J-root phase. The equality: by `mem_filteredFrom`, with `F ⪯ F′` (F-root: the tests `F′ ⪯ B ∧ F′ ⪯ B` against `F ⪯ B ∧ F ⪯ B ∧ F′ ⪯ B`; J-root: `F′ ⪯ B ∧ J ⪯ B` against `F ⪯ B ∧ J ⪯ B ∧ F′ ⪯ B`). A removed block B has a witness W in the set with `h(W) ≥ lo`. F-root: `lo > h(F′)` (`hband`), so `F′ ⪯ W` (`hext`); `B ⪯ W`; B and F′ are comparable (`preceq_total_of_common`), and not `F′ ⪯ B`, so `B ⪯ F′`. J-root: `J ⪯ B` and `F′ ⪯ J` (`hroot`) give `F′ ⪯ B`, so no block is removed. The witnesses are the set's own blocks: no carrier of F′ in the set is needed (ldp §9.3, "Missing witness", lines 641–650).
